# Rich text

`howdy/ui/rich_text` is an editor for formatted writing, like Rails' Action
Text: bold, italic, strikethrough, links, headings, quotes, lists and code,
with mentions of people or anything else, and files sent straight to
storage. It looks like the rest of your [theme](/docs/ui/themes), and the
server gets the writing as a typed Gleam value it can trust.

## Storing documents, not HTML

The editor posts a *document*: JSON describing paragraphs, headings, lists,
marks, mentions and attachments, in the shape ProseMirror uses.
`howdy/ui/rich_text/document` reads it into Gleam values:

```gleam
pub type Block {
  Paragraph(content: List(Inline))
  Heading(level: Int, content: List(Inline))
  Quote(blocks: List(Block))
  BulletList(items: List(List(Block)))
  NumberedList(start: Int, items: List(List(Block)))
  CodeBlock(code: String)
  Divider
  Attached(attachment: Attachment)
}
```

Reading is the safety check. `document.decode` refuses anything the editor
could not have made: an unknown kind of block, a `<script>` in place of
JSON, a link that is not `http`, `https`, `mailto`, `tel` or a path on your
own site, such as `javascript:alert(1)`, or a document nested deeper than
any person would write. A forged post gets no further than a typed one.

Showing it is safe too. `document.view` draws the document through Lustre,
which escapes all its text, so there is no HTML to sanitise and nothing to
forget. Store the document with `document.to_string`, in an ordinary text
column, and decode it again when you read it.

## The editor

Put the editor in a form, like any other control:

```gleam
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/button.{Primary}
import howdy/ui/rich_text

html.form([attribute.method("post"), attribute.action("/posts")], [
  ui.stack([], [
    ui.label([attribute.for("body")], [text("Post")]),
    rich_text.new(name: "body")
      |> rich_text.id("body")
      |> rich_text.value(post.body)
      |> rich_text.placeholder("Write something…")
      |> rich_text.required
      |> rich_text.view,
    ui.submit_button(Primary, [], [text("Post")]),
  ]),
])
```

The editor is a builder, used from its own module:

- `new(name:)` names the form field the document is posted as.
- `value` is the document to start with; without one the editor is empty.
- `placeholder` is shown, faintly, while the editor is empty.
- `id` ties the editor to a `<label for>`, so clicking the label focuses
  it and a screen reader announces it. Without one, the id is made from
  the name.
- `required` stops the form submitting while the editor is empty, with the
  browser's own message.
- `disabled` shows the document without letting it be edited, and posts
  nothing. `invalid` marks it as holding a value that was refused, as a
  field with an error does.
- `tools` chooses the toolbar's buttons, in groups with a line between
  each. `default_tools()` is the toolbar an editor has without it. An
  empty list hides the toolbar; the keyboard shortcuts still work.

```gleam
rich_text.new(name: "note")
|> rich_text.tools([
  [rich_text.Bold, rich_text.Italic, rich_text.Link],
  [rich_text.BulletList, rich_text.NumberedList],
])
|> rich_text.view
```

The editor understands Markdown-like shortcuts as you type: `#` for a
heading, `>` for a quote, `-` or `1.` for a list, three backticks for code,
and `**bold**`, `_italic_`, `~~strike~~` and `` `code` ``. Shift+Enter
breaks a line within a paragraph.

### The browser code

The editor runs on [ProseMirror](https://prosemirror.net), bundled into
`howdy_ui`. Mount `ui.rich_text()` in the app to serve it:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(ui.rich_text())
|> howdy.controller(pages())
```

Only pages with an editor load it, the first time one appears. Its URL
changes whenever the code does, so browsers cache it for good. Until it
loads, the page shows the document as it stands, in the same box the
editor will take, so nothing jumps. If the code cannot load, the editor
says so.

## Reading what was posted

In a form validator, `rich_text.field` reads the document and checks it
with rules, like the other [form fields](/docs/controllers/forms):

```gleam
import howdy/form.{type Form}
import howdy/ui/rich_text
import howdy/validate

fn post_from_form(fields: Form) -> validate.Result(Post) {
  use title <- form.string(fields, "title", [validate.not_empty()])
  use body <- rich_text.field(fields, "body", [
    rich_text.not_empty(),
    rich_text.max_length(10_000),
  ])
  validate.ok(Post(title:, body:))
}
```

A field that fails reports a [problem](/docs/i18n/overview), which a page
puts into words in the reader's language like any other:

- A missing field is `problem.Required`.
- A value that is not a document the editor could have made is
  `problem.Custom("not_rich_text", "is not valid rich text")`, which keeps
  its English message; a form that came from the editor never gets it.
- `not_empty` refuses a document with no text, mentions, attachments or
  dividers, as `problem.Blank`. Check it on the server even with
  `required`, since a browser can be told anything.
- `max_length` refuses a document whose plain text is longer than a number
  of characters, as `problem.TooLong`.

Outside a form, `document.decode` reads a document from a string, and
`document.decoder()` reads one inside other JSON. Their errors say what
was wrong, for your logs; they are not for people.

To bring an existing plain text column into rich text, `document.from_text`
makes a paragraph for each run of lines separated by a blank line.

## Mentions

Typing a trigger, such as `@`, suggests things to mention as the writer
types. Each trigger has its own search:

```gleam
rich_text.new(name: "body")
|> rich_text.mentions(trigger: "@", kind: "person", search: "/people/search")
|> rich_text.mentions(trigger: "#", kind: "issue", search: "/issues/search")
|> rich_text.view
```

The editor asks the `search` endpoint for what follows the trigger, as
`?q=`, such as `GET /people/search?q=ad`. Answer with
`rich_text.suggestions_to_json`, best first, ten or so at most:

```gleam
fn search_people(ctx: Context) {
  use q <- query.string_or(ctx, "q", default: "")
  let found =
    people.search(db, q, limit: 10)
    |> list.map(fn(person) {
      rich_text.Suggestion(
        id: person.id,
        label: person.name,
        detail: Some(person.email),
        image: Some(person.avatar_url),
      )
    })
  controller.json(ctx, rich_text.suggestions_to_json(found))
}
```

`label` is what the mention shows after its trigger, such as
`@Ada Lovelace`. `detail` and `image` appear in the list of suggestions
only. Guard the endpoint as you would any other: it tells whoever asks who
there is to mention.

A mention keeps its trigger, its kind, the id you gave it and the label
shown when it was made. `document.mentions` lists everyone and everything
mentioned, once each, in order. The ids came from a browser, so check each
against your own records before acting on it, such as before telling
someone they were mentioned with [notifications](/docs/notifications/overview):

```gleam
use person <- list.each(document.mentions(post.body))
case person.kind, people.may_mention(db, author, person.id) {
  "person", True -> notify_mentioned(person.id, post)
  _, _ -> Nil
}
```

## Attachments

Writers can attach files by the toolbar, by pasting or by dropping them.
The browser sends each file straight to storage, with progress, and the
document keeps its key:

```gleam
rich_text.new(name: "body")
|> rich_text.attachments(start: "/posts/uploads", url: fn(file) {
  option.from_result(storage.url(files, file.key, expires_in: 3600))
})
|> rich_text.accept(["image/*", ".pdf"])
|> rich_text.max_size(10_000_000)
|> rich_text.view
```

`start` is the same kind of endpoint as the
[uploader's](/docs/ui/components#Uploads): the browser posts it each file's
name, type and size, and it answers with a key and where to send the file,
which `howdy_storage` gives you; see
[direct uploads](/docs/storage/overview#Direct-uploads). Check the type and
size there too: `accept` and `max_size` only stop the browser sending files
it should not. `url` gives the address to show an attached file by, so
images appear in the editor, or `None` to show only its name. The form
will not submit while a file is still on its way.

`document.attachments` lists every attached file. Check each key is one
your app handed to this writer, such as by its prefix, before you keep the
document, as with any [direct upload](/docs/storage/overview#Direct-uploads).

## Showing a document

`document.view` draws a document as prose, in the theme's prose styles,
with mentions as chips and attachments as cards:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/rich_text/document

html.article([], [document.view(post.body)])
```

A mention's label and an attachment's name are what they were when the
document was written. As with Action Text, draw them from your records as
they are now with `document.view_with`, so a renamed person shows their
new name and a deleted file shows as gone:

```gleam
document.view_with(
  post.body,
  mention: fn(mention) {
    case people.get(db, mention.id) {
      Ok(person) ->
        document.mention_view(
          document.Mention(..mention, label: person.name),
          href: Some("/people/" <> person.id),
        )
      Error(_) -> document.mention_view(mention, href: None)
    }
  },
  attachment: fn(file) {
    let url = storage.url(files, file.key, expires_in: 3600)
    document.attachment_view(file, url: option.from_result(url))
  },
)
```

`document.attachment_view` shows an image as a picture when it has a
`url`, and any other file as a card with its name and size, linked to the
file when there is a `url`. `document.elements` gives the document's
elements without the prose around them, for a container of your own.

For search, previews, notifications and email, `document.to_plain_text`
gives the text: paragraphs separated by blank lines, list items marked `-`
or `1.`, quotes with `>`, mentions as `@Ada Lovelace` and attachments as
their names in brackets. `document.is_empty` says whether there is
anything worth keeping.

## In a live view

In a [live component](/docs/ui/live-components),
`live.on_value(name, message)` on any element around the editor hears the
document, as JSON, each time the editor loses focus:

```gleam
html.div([live.on_value("body", BodyChanged)], [
  rich_text.new(name: "body") |> rich_text.value(model.body) |> rich_text.view,
])
```

Decode it with `document.decode` in `update`. Giving the editor a
different `value` from the server, such as an empty document after a
message is sent, replaces what is in it; re-rendering with the value it
already has leaves the writer undisturbed.

## Words and other languages

The editor's labels, from the toolbar's buttons to its upload errors, are
the `rich_text` field of Howdy UI's `howdy/ui/words.Words`, a
`RichTextWords` record. The editor speaks English unless given words with
`rich_text.words`, which takes the whole `Words`, as `data_table.words`
does:

```gleam
let w = words.translated(t)

rich_text.new(name: "body")
|> rich_text.words(w)
|> rich_text.view
```

`words.translated` has the editor's words in every language Howdy UI
translates; see [internationalisation](/docs/i18n/overview). To change a
few, update the record. `heading` may contain `{level}`, and `too_large`
`{limit}`, which the editor fills in:

```gleam
let w = words.english()
let w =
  words.Words(
    ..w,
    rich_text: words.RichTextWords(..w.rich_text, strike: "Strike out"),
  )
```

## Copying the editor

`gleam run -m howdy/ui add rich_text` copies the editor's Gleam source into
your project to change, as with any [component](/docs/ui/components). The
copy still loads its browser code, and the document module, from
`howdy_ui`, so keep `ui.rich_text()` mounted.
